<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/expert/dark-matter-and-dark-energy">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    The earlier tiers built everything you need to audit the invisible. <em>Galaxies and the
    Expanding Universe</em> (advanced) introduced galactic rotation and the distance ladder;
    <em>Compact Objects</em> (advanced) supplied white dwarfs and the supernova physics behind
    the standard candles; <em>The Milky Way</em> (intermediate) showed how the 21-cm line
    traces hydrogen clouds orbiting our own galaxy. This lesson runs that machinery on the
    95% of the cosmos that emits nothing at all.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>run the rotation-curve argument: what Keplerian gravity predicts, what galaxies do, and what the discrepancy requires;</li>
    <li>explain how Type Ia supernovae turned the expansion's acceleration into a measurement in 1998;</li>
    <li>deploy the Bullet Cluster and the CMB's acoustic peaks to rule out dim ordinary matter;</li>
    <li>reason from composition to fate, and say what the parameter w and its evolution decide;</li>
    <li>weigh the particle candidates and the modified-gravity alternative honestly, given four decades of null results.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Everything in this atlas — every atom, star and lesson — describes <strong>about 5%
    of the universe</strong>. The other 95% is dark: <strong>dark matter</strong> (~27%),
    whose gravity holds galaxies together but which no telescope has seen, and
    <strong>dark energy</strong> (~68%), which accelerates the expansion and whose nature
    is a total mystery. This is not a crisis for cosmology; it is the frontier that keeps
    it honest. The syllabus's cosmology topics — recession and redshift, age and size,
    the Big Bang's evidence, the fate of the universe — are exactly the measurements
    that exposed both darks, and this page follows each topic through the lens of what
    it forced cosmologists to conclude about the invisible 95%.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two darks — what we know, and the honest size of the gap" widget="equationStep" config={darkCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards split cleanly into measurement and mystery.
    Cards 1 and 2 are the gravity audit, two independent ways of weighing what cannot be
    seen — motion first, then bent light — and card 2's Bullet Cluster does something more:
    it separates the dark mass from the hot gas, ruling out ordinary matter in hiding. Card 3
    is the supernova measurement that forced dark energy into the model. Only card 4 is
    conjecture — and notice how bare it is. The same logic once pointed from Uranus's
    misbehaving orbit to Neptune's position before anyone saw it; here the deviation is
    measured but the "Neptune" is still missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> "95% unknown" is a measurement, not a confession — the inventory
    comes from gravitational effects, the one signal darkness cannot hide. The first place
    dark energy surfaced was the distance–redshift relationship itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Recession, redshift and the discovery of acceleration</h2>
  <p>
    The distance–redshift relationship is where dark energy was found. In the 1990s two
    teams (Perlmutter's Supernova Cosmology Project; Schmidt, Riess and the High-Z
    Supernova Search) used Type Ia supernovae as standard candles — the exploding
    white dwarf's brightness is calibrated by its light-curve shape — to extend the
    Hubble diagram to billions of light-years. The result (1998, Nobel 2011): distant
    supernovae are <em>fainter</em> — farther — than any decelerating or even coasting
    universe predicts. The expansion is speeding up. In the Friedmann equations,
    acceleration requires a component with negative effective pressure: the
    cosmological constant Λ (Einstein's "biggest blunder", resurrected) fits — a
    constant energy density, ~68% of the universe, whose density does not dilute as
    space grows. The worked comparison: a supernova at redshift z = 0.5 appears about
    25% fainter than in a matter-only universe — a difference of a few tenths of a
    magnitude, detectable only because the calibration is that good. Dark energy is
    named, not explained: "dark" because it does not emit or absorb, "energy" because
    it behaves like one — a property of empty space itself, whatever that means.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> acceleration was not a speculation but a residual — distant
    supernovae fainter than any decelerating or coasting universe allows, detectable only
    because standard-candle calibration is that good. The next question is whether the dark
    components that fit the expansion also fit the universe's age, size and geometry.
  </p>

  <h2>Age, size and the concordance numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The age and size of the universe are where the dark components earn their keep in
    the arithmetic. A matter-only universe of the measured density would be younger
    than its own oldest stars (about 10 billion years — an absurdity resolved only
    when the dark components entered the fit); with the measured mix — roughly 5%
    baryons, 27% dark matter, 68% dark energy — the Friedmann equations give 13.8
    billion years, consistent with the oldest globular clusters and stellar
    chronometry. The same fit pins the geometry flat, the expansion rate, and the
    matter fraction independently from the CMB's acoustic peaks. The dark matter
    fraction itself was history's slow reveal: Zwicky's 1933 observation that the
    Coma cluster's galaxies move too fast for their visible mass ("dunkle Materie"),
    Rubin and Ford's 1970s galaxy rotation curves, and finally the CMB — where the
    acoustic peak heights split matter into baryonic (interacting) and dark
    (non-interacting) components cleanly. The concordance is the point: three
    instruments — supernova light curves, galaxy dynamics, the microwave sky — agree
    on the inventory to within a few percent, which is why "95% unknown" is a
    measurement, not a confession.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rotation-curve arithmetic is the clearest single demonstration. In a
    Keplerian system (all mass central, as in the Solar System), orbital speed
    falls with radius as v = √(GM/r) — Mercury orbits faster than Neptune. Galaxies
    disobey: measuring the orbital speed of hydrogen clouds (via the 21-cm line)
    versus radius, Rubin and Ford found the curves flat — speeds near 200–300 km/s
    maintained far beyond the visible disk, where the Keplerian prediction (with
    all visible mass counted) would fall by half or more. The discrepancy is not
    subtle: matching a flat curve at 30 kiloparsecs requires roughly 5–6 times the
    luminous mass, arranged in a halo extending far beyond the starlight. The
    dark halo is invisible precisely because it is dark — and it is the galaxy
    we actually live in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "dark energy is a pushing force, so space is
    expanding inside the Solar System, inside galaxies, inside us." Dark energy's
    effect scales with distance, and inside gravitationally bound systems its
    influence is roughly a million-billionth of gravity — the Earth, the Solar
    System, the Milky Way and the Local Group are not expanding and never will be
    (short of a Big Rip scenario, which requires a dark component that strengthens,
    not the constant we measure). Expansion applies to unbound space: the emptiness
    between galaxy clusters. You are not, repeat not, being slowly inflated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the rotation curves force a dark halo, and the concordance of
    supernovae, galaxy dynamics and the microwave sky fixes the inventory to within a few
    percent. What remains is to see the dark matter <em>working</em> — in structure formation,
    where it turns out to have been a silent partner in the Big Bang's evidence all along.
  </p>

  <h2>The Big Bang evidence and dark matter's hand in it</h2>
  <p>
    Dark matter was a silent partner in the Big Bang's evidence. Structure formation is
    the deepest link: the CMB shows density fluctuations of one part in 100,000 at
    380,000 years — far too small, in a baryon-only universe, to grow into galaxies by
    today (baryons could not even clump before that epoch, since radiation pressure
    held them up). Dark matter, interacting only gravitationally, could begin
    collapsing <em>before</em> the CMB was released — building gravitational wells into
    which the baryons then fell, amplified. Without it, the galaxies we observe could
    not exist: the simulations (hierarchical clumping of cold dark matter) reproduce
    the observed cosmic web — filaments, walls, voids — and predict the CMB peak
    structure measured by Planck. Then the Bullet Cluster (1E 0657−56) made the case
    visual: two colliding clusters, whose hot gas (most of the normal matter, slammed
    to a halt in the collision) is visibly <em>separated</em> from the mass that keeps
    gravitational lensing in place — dark matter passing through itself and the gas,
    exactly as a weakly-interacting component should. It is the closest thing the
    field has to a photograph of the dark sector.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "dark matter is just normal matter that's hard to
    see — dim stars, rogue planets, dust." The baryonic inventory is measured: Big
    Bang nucleosynthesis plus the CMB fix the total baryon density at about 15% of the
    matter density, and surveys of stars, gas and compact objects account for most of
    it (the "missing baryons" problem was eventually solved — they hide in
    ionised intergalactic filaments). Dark matter is five to six times the baryonic
    total and demonstrably non-baryonic: it neither collides like gas (Bullet Cluster)
    nor couples to the photon-baryon plasma before recombination (CMB acoustic peaks).
    MACHOs are out; the search is for a new particle or a new law.
  </p>

  <h2>Dark matter, dark energy and the fate question</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's fate topic is decided by the darks. The dark-energy density (if
    truly constant) wins increasingly against gravity as space dilutes the matter:
    expansion accelerates forever; the Local Group still merges gravitationally, but
    every other galaxy cluster eventually crosses the cosmic horizon — future
    astronomers (if any) would see only their own galaxy in a black sky. The deep
    future: star formation exhausts gas within about 100 trillion years, the stellar
    era ends, and the universe drifts toward the cold near-void of the heat death.
    The alternatives depend on dark energy's equation of state: if its density grows
    (w below −1), expansion tears apart galaxies, then star systems, then atoms — the
    Big Rip; if it decays, recollapse becomes possible again. The measurement task is
    correspondingly precise: constrain w and its time evolution with supernova
    distances, weak gravitational lensing, baryon acoustic oscillations (the sound-
    horizon scale imprinted on galaxy clustering — DESI mapped tens of millions of
    galaxies against it) and the CMB. The 2024–25 DESI results added the live twist:
    hints, at two-to-four sigma depending on the supernova dataset combined, that the
    dark component's density <em>decreases</em> over time — tentative, contested, and
    the first serious challenge to the cosmological constant in its four-decade reign.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the cosmological constant is just Einstein's old
    fudge factor — a number invented to make the equations fit, so it is probably an
    artefact." True as history (Einstein added Λ in 1917 for a static universe and
    allegedly regretted it), false as science: today the same Λ is required
    <em>independently</em> by supernova distances, by the CMB's peak structure, by
    baryon acoustic oscillations and by the universe's measured flatness — four
    datasets, one value, no freedom. A fudge factor reconciles one dataset with
    itself; this one unifies datasets that share no apparatus. The mystery is not
    whether Λ fits (it does, eerily well) but why its measured value is so absurdly
    small compared with quantum-field-theory estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recount the rotation-curve argument: what does a Keplerian system predict for orbital speed versus radius, what do real galaxies do, and what does the discrepancy require?</li>
    <li>A colleague proposes that dark matter is just dim stars, rogue planets and dust. Cite two independent measurements from this lesson that rule that out.</li>
    <li>Why does the lesson argue that the cosmological constant is not a fudge factor, despite Einstein having introduced it as one?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) In a Keplerian system with all mass central, orbital speed
    falls with radius as v = √(GM/r) — Mercury outruns Neptune. Galaxies instead hold speeds
    near 200–300 km/s far beyond the visible disk, and matching a flat curve at 30 kiloparsecs
    requires roughly 5–6 times the luminous mass, arranged in a halo extending far beyond the
    starlight. (2) First, the baryonic inventory: Big Bang nucleosynthesis plus the CMB fix
    ordinary matter at about 15% of the matter density, and dark matter is five to six times
    the baryonic total — the dim stuff cannot mass enough even in principle. Second, the
    Bullet Cluster: the mass that lenses light is visibly separated from the hot gas and
    passed through the collision like a weakly-interacting component, which no cloud of dim
    stars or dust would do. (3) A fudge factor reconciles one dataset with itself. Today's Λ
    is required independently by supernova distances, the CMB's peak structure, baryon
    acoustic oscillations and the measured flatness — four datasets, one value, no freedom.
    The real mystery is not whether Λ fits but why its measured value is so absurdly small
    compared with quantum-field-theory estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The 95% dark inventory is a measurement: supernovae, galaxy dynamics and the microwave sky agree to within a few percent.</li>
    <li>Flat rotation curves at 200–300 km/s force a dark halo of roughly 5–6 times the luminous mass.</li>
    <li>Type Ia supernovae made acceleration measurable: distant ones are fainter than any decelerating universe allows.</li>
    <li>The Bullet Cluster separates lensing mass from hot gas — dark matter is demonstrably non-baryonic.</li>
    <li>Dark matter could collapse before the CMB was released, building the wells that seeded every galaxy.</li>
    <li>Fate follows composition: a constant dark energy gives accelerating expansion toward heat death; a strengthening one gives the Big Rip.</li>
    <li>Four decades of direct searches have found nothing — dark matter is stranger, weaker or lighter than the leading guess.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Cosmology and General Relativity</em> holds the frame this lesson filled in — the
    ΛCDM model, the Friedmann equations and the Hubble tension the darks feed into — and
    <em>Astrobiology</em> asks what the model's long, accelerating future means for the one
    biosphere we know about. The darks are the open edge; the rest of the atlas stands on
    the measured 5%.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The direct detection effort.</strong> If dark matter is a particle, three
    hunt modes run in parallel. <em>Direct detection</em>: underground detectors —
    XENONnT in Italy, LZ in South Dakota, PandaX in China — litres of liquid xenon a
    kilometre down, watching for a nuclear recoil as a WIMP passes through; decades of
    results have excluded huge parameter space and found nothing, pushing the
    interaction limit below any natural expectation. <em>Indirect detection</em>:
    telescopes watching the galactic centre and dwarf galaxies for annihilation
    photons (Fermi-LAT), positron excesses (the AMS-02 debate) — signals seen, none
    confirmed above background. <em>Production</em>: the LHC can make dark-sector
    particles if they weigh less than its reach — searches for missing energy and
    long-lived particles, so far null. The candidate pool has shifted accordingly:
    WIMPs (weakened), axions (revived — ultralight, predicted for an unrelated reason
    (the strong CP problem), detectable in principle by resonant cavities: ADMX),
    sterile neutrinos, primordial black holes (constrained by microlensing and the
    CMB), and "dark photon"/hidden-sector models. The lesson of forty years of null
    results: dark matter is stranger, weaker or lighter than the leading guess.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Modified gravity: the alternative, honestly.</strong> MOND (Milgrom, 1983)
    modifies Newtonian dynamics below a critical acceleration, and its track record
    deserves respect: it fits galaxy rotation curves with essentially one parameter —
    including predicting tight empirical relations (the baryonic Tully–Fisher law)
    that cold dark matter struggles to explain. But it fails where the data are
    decisive: cluster lensing (the Bullet Cluster's offset mass), the CMB's acoustic
    peak heights (which need a non-baryonic component even with modified dynamics),
    structure-formation timing, and gravitational-wave speed (GW170817's photons and
    waves arriving within seconds of each other killed most relativistic MOND
    variants). The live research position: dark matter with extra galactic-scale
    physics (self-interacting dark matter, fuzzy dark matter) versus hybrid
    frameworks. The dark matter case is strong; the honest field keeps testing the
    alternative rather than declaring it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dark energy: the conceptual problem.</strong> The cosmological constant's
    value is the worst prediction in physics: quantum field theory naïvely estimates
    vacuum energy about 10¹²⁰ times larger than observed — and the observed value is
    also suspiciously tuned: large enough to dominate now, small enough not to have
    prevented galaxy formation earlier. The proposals: a true vacuum constant (why so
    small? — anthropic/landscape arguments, controversial); quintessence (a dynamic
    field, generically time-varying — exactly what DESI's hints probe); modified
    gravity on cosmic scales (which must also pass the Solar System's tight GR tests);
    or an apparent effect of cosmic inhomogeneity (the backreaction idea, mostly
    argued against but not dead). The next decade of surveys — DESI, Rubin/LSST
    (billions of galaxies, week-scale transient discovery), Euclid, Roman — is
    designed to settle whether w evolves. Cosmology's 95% problem is not a gap in the
    textbooks; it is the active edge of physics, measured to percent precision and
    understood not at all — which is what makes it the best kind of problem.
  </p>
